1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:03,236
It is the night of November second,
nineteen oh-seven.

2
00:00:04,036 --> 00:00:06,298
New York is on the edge of collapse.

3
00:00:07,096 --> 00:00:11,689
Banks are failing one after another, and
thousands of people are standing in the
streets,

4
00:00:11,823 --> 00:00:13,303
demanding their money back.

5
00:00:14,102 --> 00:00:22,847
At the heart of Manhattan, in a quiet
marble library, the most powerful banker
on earth is waiting for the city's richest
men to walk through his doors.

6
00:00:23,646 --> 00:00:26,858
When they arrive, he does something no one
expects.

7
00:00:27,656 --> 00:00:33,993
He locks the door, puts the key in his
pocket, and tells them: nobody leaves
until the country is saved.

8
00:00:34,792 --> 00:00:37,014
The government could not stop the panic.

9
00:00:37,812 --> 00:00:39,566
The president could not stop it.

10
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:41,884
Only one man could.

11
00:00:42,682 --> 00:00:44,905
His name was John Pierpont Morgan.

12
00:00:45,703 --> 00:00:48,121
The panic is already in its second week.

13
00:00:48,919 --> 00:00:52,483
Interest rates on emergency loans have hit
one hundred percent.

14
00:00:53,281 --> 00:00:58,889
The stock market is collapsing, and the
Knickerbocker Trust Company, the third
largest in the city,

15
00:00:59,115 --> 00:01:03,290
has just shut its doors, with thousands of
depositors still outside.

16
00:01:04,089 --> 00:01:06,324
Everyone is looking for someone to blame.

17
00:01:07,122 --> 00:01:09,501
Everyone is looking for someone to save
them.

18
00:01:10,299 --> 00:01:14,345
But the man who saved America that night
also owned a frightening share of it.

19
00:01:15,143 --> 00:01:20,478
This is the story of how one banker became
the most powerful private citizen in
American history,

20
00:01:20,690 --> 00:01:24,045
and why the country ended up fearing the
very man who rescued it.

21
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:31,303
John Pierpont Morgan was born on April
seventeenth, eighteen thirty-seven, in
Hartford, Connecticut.

22
00:01:32,104 --> 00:01:36,917
Unlike Rockefeller, Vanderbilt or
Carnegie, he did not start with nothing.

23
00:01:37,716 --> 00:01:42,881
His father, Junius Morgan, was a rich
merchant who became a partner of George
Peabody,

24
00:01:43,093 --> 00:01:47,386
the American banker who ran one of the
most powerful financial houses in London.

25
00:01:48,184 --> 00:01:51,696
Money, to the Morgan family, was not
something you chased.

26
00:01:52,494 --> 00:01:54,157
It was the family business.

27
00:01:54,955 --> 00:02:00,576
Young Pierpont was a sickly child, often
in bed with fevers, but he was a sharp
student,

28
00:02:00,697 --> 00:02:02,230
especially with numbers.

29
00:02:03,028 --> 00:02:10,706
His father sent him to the best schools in
Boston, Switzerland and Germany, where he
learned languages and learned how Europe
moved its money.

30
00:02:11,505 --> 00:02:16,566
At twenty, he came back to New York and
went to work at a firm tied to his
father's bank.

31
00:02:17,364 --> 00:02:21,930
He was tall, proud and impatient, with a
temper that could silence a room.

32
00:02:22,729 --> 00:02:26,982
When he was seventeen, his father moved to
London to run the Peabody bank,

33
00:02:27,143 --> 00:02:28,922
and the family crossed the ocean.

34
00:02:29,721 --> 00:02:35,264
He spent his teenage years in Europe,
studied mathematics at the University of
Göttingen in Germany,

35
00:02:35,463 --> 00:02:39,300
and learned that a banker's word was only
worth what other bankers believed.

36
00:02:40,098 --> 00:02:42,529
He was also a man who knew loss early.

37
00:02:43,328 --> 00:02:47,516
In eighteen sixty-one he married a young
woman named Amelia Sturges.

38
00:02:48,315 --> 00:02:53,284
She was already ill with tuberculosis, and
she died only four months after the
wedding,

39
00:02:53,471 --> 00:02:54,834
leaving him devastated.

40
00:02:55,632 --> 00:02:57,724
Many who knew him said it hardened him.

41
00:02:58,523 --> 00:03:04,118
He threw himself into work, and he never
again let anyone see the soft side of
Pierpont Morgan.

42
00:03:04,916 --> 00:03:06,422
And he had a rare gift.

43
00:03:07,221 --> 00:03:11,800
He understood that in finance, the most
valuable currency is not gold.

44
00:03:12,598 --> 00:03:13,675
It is trust.

45
00:03:14,473 --> 00:03:17,373
His first real test would come in the
middle of a war.

46
00:03:18,294 --> 00:03:21,389
In eighteen sixty-one, the Civil War
began.

47
00:03:22,189 --> 00:03:24,724
Thousands of young men marched off to
fight.

48
00:03:25,523 --> 00:03:31,443
Morgan, like many wealthy men at the time,
paid three hundred dollars for a
substitute to take his place,

49
00:03:31,617 --> 00:03:35,258
which was perfectly legal, and he stayed
in New York to make money.

50
00:03:36,057 --> 00:03:40,154
He did make money, and one of his first
deals still follows his name.

51
00:03:40,952 --> 00:03:47,381
A speculator offered to sell the Army five
thousand carbines that the government
itself had sold off as obsolete.

52
00:03:48,179 --> 00:03:54,112
Morgan lent the money for the purchase,
and the guns were resold to the military
at several times the price.

53
00:03:54,911 --> 00:03:59,763
The rifles were dangerous, and a
Congressional committee later called the
sale shameless.

54
00:04:00,562 --> 00:04:04,464
But Morgan had only financed it, and he
walked away untouched.

55
00:04:05,262 --> 00:04:06,730
It was an early lesson.

56
00:04:07,528 --> 00:04:10,844
In wartime, a banker could profit while
others bled.

57
00:04:11,643 --> 00:04:14,881
And a banker who stayed in the background
could avoid the blame.

58
00:04:15,679 --> 00:04:23,006
After the war, he built his own firm, and
in eighteen seventy-one he joined Anthony
Drexel in Philadelphia to create Drexel,

59
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:28,279
Morgan and Company, a house with offices
in New York, Philadelphia and London.

60
00:04:29,077 --> 00:04:30,805
His routine was famous.

61
00:04:31,603 --> 00:04:37,524
He arrived at his office every morning,
sat at an open desk in the middle of the
floor so everyone could see him,

62
00:04:37,723 --> 00:04:40,532
and smoked a dozen or more large cigars a
day.

63
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:43,357
He was also known for his face.

64
00:04:44,156 --> 00:04:50,844
A skin disease had swollen his nose into a
bulbous, purple shape, and the sight of it
made strangers look away.

65
00:04:51,643 --> 00:04:56,547
Morgan hated being photographed, and his
photographers learned to retouch every
portrait.

66
00:04:57,346 --> 00:05:01,300
But when he looked at you, people said,
you forgot everything else.

67
00:05:02,098 --> 00:05:06,222
And more and more, the most powerful men
in the country were coming to him.

68
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:12,667
From then on, Morgan preferred to work
quietly, through deals, committees and
boardrooms.

69
00:05:13,465 --> 00:05:17,667
And soon he found the industry that would
make him famous: the railroads.

70
00:05:18,582 --> 00:05:22,419
Railroads were the great business of the
age, but they were a mess.

71
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:30,859
Dozens of competing lines, wild stock
manipulation, and fortunes made and
destroyed by men like Jay Gould and Jim
Fisk.

72
00:05:31,657 --> 00:05:35,598
In eighteen sixty-nine, Morgan got his
first taste of that war.

73
00:05:36,397 --> 00:05:41,744
Gould and Fisk tried to steal a small
railroad he was backing, the Albany and
Susquehanna,

74
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:44,739
using bribed judges and a stolen board
room.

75
00:05:45,537 --> 00:05:49,049
Morgan fought back, took the fight to
court, and won.

76
00:05:49,847 --> 00:05:51,353
The lesson stayed with him.

77
00:05:52,152 --> 00:05:56,445
Chaos in the railroads was bad for
investors, and bad for business.

78
00:05:57,243 --> 00:06:02,877
So Morgan began buying, merging and
reorganizing failing lines under strict
control.

79
00:06:03,675 --> 00:06:05,403
The method became famous.

80
00:06:06,201 --> 00:06:13,085
He would take over a bankrupt railroad,
cut its debt, replace its managers, and
put his own partners on the board.

81
00:06:13,884 --> 00:06:16,588
Wall Street gave it a name: Morganization.

82
00:06:17,386 --> 00:06:18,840
His method was simple.

83
00:06:19,639 --> 00:06:23,515
Before he agreed to rescue a railroad, he
demanded control.

84
00:06:24,313 --> 00:06:30,755
He took seats on the board, he chose the
new managers, and he kept the bonds in the
hands of investors he trusted.

85
00:06:31,553 --> 00:06:36,054
The investors loved him for it, because
his railroads stopped going bankrupt.

86
00:06:36,852 --> 00:06:40,598
The railroad barons hated him, because he
told them what to do.

87
00:06:41,397 --> 00:06:44,544
The Vanderbilt deal showed how far his
reach had grown.

88
00:06:45,342 --> 00:06:51,145
The shares were worth about twenty-five
million dollars, one of the largest stock
sales ever attempted.

89
00:06:51,944 --> 00:06:56,757
Morgan handled it in secret, so the market
never panicked, and the sale went through.

90
00:06:57,556 --> 00:07:02,304
From then on, a rule spread through Wall
Street: if you wanted to raise real money,

91
00:07:02,543 --> 00:07:03,971
you went to Morgan first.

92
00:07:04,769 --> 00:07:08,163
In eighteen seventy-nine, he proved his
power again.

93
00:07:08,962 --> 00:07:14,596
When William Vanderbilt needed to sell a
huge block of New York Central stock
without crashing the market,

94
00:07:14,795 --> 00:07:15,794
he called Morgan.

95
00:07:16,592 --> 00:07:21,679
The banker placed the shares quietly with
investors in London, and kept a seat on
the board.

96
00:07:22,477 --> 00:07:24,674
Morgan was no longer a young banker.

97
00:07:25,472 --> 00:07:29,231
He was becoming the man who decided who
ran America's railroads.

98
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:37,594
Morgan's partnership with the Drexel
family of Philadelphia gave him a global
reach, and in eighteen ninety-five the
firm became J.P.

99
00:07:37,507 --> 00:07:38,714
Morgan and Company.

100
00:07:39,514 --> 00:07:41,424
His next target was the future.

101
00:07:42,223 --> 00:07:48,182
In the eighteen eighties, Thomas Edison
was bringing electric light to the world,
and Edison needed money.

102
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:52,296
Morgan became one of his first and most
important backers.

103
00:07:53,095 --> 00:07:58,885
His own home on Madison Avenue was among
the first private homes in New York wired
for electricity.

104
00:07:59,683 --> 00:08:04,914
Legend says the lights sometimes sparked,
and Morgan, unbothered, kept them on.

105
00:08:05,712 --> 00:08:11,020
In eighteen ninety-two, he engineered the
merger of Edison's company with its
biggest rival.

106
00:08:11,819 --> 00:08:15,135
The result had a simple new name: General
Electric.

107
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:17,635
That was the Morgan formula.

108
00:08:18,433 --> 00:08:24,458
Find an industry full of fierce rivals,
bring everyone to the table, merge them
into one giant,

109
00:08:24,631 --> 00:08:26,203
and take a seat on the board.

110
00:08:27,001 --> 00:08:30,786
The revolution began on September fourth,
eighteen eighty-two.

111
00:08:31,584 --> 00:08:36,476
Edison turned on the Pearl Street Station,
the first commercial power plant in New
York,

112
00:08:36,689 --> 00:08:39,393
and light flooded a small part of lower
Manhattan.

113
00:08:40,191 --> 00:08:43,794
Among the first customers was the Drexel
Morgan office itself.

114
00:08:44,592 --> 00:08:50,747
Morgan, who had put his own money into the
venture, stood in the new glow and
understood the future had arrived.

115
00:08:51,545 --> 00:08:59,198
He also understood something that the
inventors did not: electricity would only
become a great industry if it stopped
being a war of patents.

116
00:08:59,996 --> 00:09:01,828
So he did what he did best.

117
00:09:02,626 --> 00:09:05,630
He made peace, and he made himself the
referee.

118
00:09:06,428 --> 00:09:10,929
When the new General Electric Company was
born, it had the biggest rival absorbed,

119
00:09:11,103 --> 00:09:14,393
the patents combined, and a Morgan partner
on the board.

120
00:09:15,191 --> 00:09:17,075
Critics called him a monopolist.

121
00:09:17,874 --> 00:09:19,289
Morgan called it order.

122
00:09:20,087 --> 00:09:22,935
Competition, he believed, was waste.

123
00:09:23,733 --> 00:09:28,221
And he was about to test that belief on
the greatest crisis the country had yet
faced,

124
00:09:28,433 --> 00:09:31,828
one that threatened to leave the American
government itself bankrupt.

125
00:09:32,742 --> 00:09:36,553
By eighteen ninety-five, America was
running out of gold.

126
00:09:37,354 --> 00:09:44,407
The country was on the gold standard, and
the Treasury's reserves had fallen so low
that the government was days away from
defaulting.

127
00:09:45,205 --> 00:09:47,506
President Grover Cleveland was desperate.

128
00:09:48,304 --> 00:09:50,032
Congress refused to act.

129
00:09:50,830 --> 00:09:54,524
And so the president of the United States
turned to a private banker.

130
00:09:55,322 --> 00:09:58,040
Morgan arrived in Washington and brought a
plan.

131
00:09:58,838 --> 00:10:04,472
His syndicate would supply sixty-five
million dollars in gold, in exchange for
government bonds,

132
00:10:04,671 --> 00:10:07,714
and the banks would work to keep the gold
inside the country.

133
00:10:08,512 --> 00:10:09,941
Cleveland hesitated.

134
00:10:10,739 --> 00:10:13,665
Taking help from Wall Street was
politically toxic.

135
00:10:14,463 --> 00:10:15,969
But there was no choice.

136
00:10:16,768 --> 00:10:20,331
Morgan's syndicate held the line, and the
gold stopped draining.

137
00:10:21,130 --> 00:10:22,597
The country was saved.

138
00:10:23,395 --> 00:10:26,165
And the banker made a handsome profit from
doing it.

139
00:10:26,963 --> 00:10:28,404
The press was divided.

140
00:10:29,203 --> 00:10:30,904
Some called him a patriot.

141
00:10:31,703 --> 00:10:38,066
Others asked a more uncomfortable
question: what kind of country depends on
one man's bank account to stay alive?

142
00:10:38,864 --> 00:10:40,331
The numbers were frightening.

143
00:10:41,130 --> 00:10:45,982
The Treasury believed it needed at least
one hundred million dollars in gold to
stay safe.

144
00:10:46,781 --> 00:10:54,159
By early eighteen ninety-five, the reserve
had fallen to about nine million, and gold
was draining out of the country every day.

145
00:10:54,958 --> 00:10:57,323
Morgan took the night train to Washington.

146
00:10:58,122 --> 00:11:03,743
The Treasury Secretary was reluctant, and
the president could barely hide his
distaste for Wall Street.

147
00:11:04,541 --> 00:11:06,243
But Morgan did not argue.

148
00:11:07,041 --> 00:11:12,441
He told the president he knew of a twelve
million dollar draft that would hit the
Treasury the very next day,

149
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:14,316
and that it would empty the reserve.

150
00:11:15,114 --> 00:11:22,454
When Cleveland finally agreed, Morgan and
his partners sold bonds to investors
across the world and brought gold back to
the Treasury,

151
00:11:22,588 --> 00:11:24,745
which kept the dollar on the gold
standard.

152
00:11:25,544 --> 00:11:27,336
It was an extraordinary moment.

153
00:11:28,135 --> 00:11:33,599
The most powerful nation in the world had
needed help, and it was a private citizen
who had provided it.

154
00:11:34,398 --> 00:11:36,477
Morgan did not wait for an answer.

155
00:11:37,275 --> 00:11:43,547
He was already looking at an even bigger
prize, the one that would make him the
most powerful man in American business.

156
00:11:44,478 --> 00:11:49,266
At the turn of the century, Andrew
Carnegie owned the largest steel company
in the world,

157
00:11:49,465 --> 00:11:51,063
and he was ready to retire.

158
00:11:51,863 --> 00:11:53,434
Morgan was ready to buy.

159
00:11:54,246 --> 00:12:00,375
In nineteen oh-one, Carnegie scribbled a
price on a slip of paper: four hundred and
eighty million dollars.

160
00:12:01,173 --> 00:12:03,968
Morgan glanced at it and said: "I accept."

161
00:12:04,767 --> 00:12:06,156
No negotiation.

162
00:12:06,954 --> 00:12:07,992
No haggling.

163
00:12:08,790 --> 00:12:14,580
Morgan merged Carnegie's company with
several others into a single colossus:
United States Steel,

164
00:12:14,767 --> 00:12:17,354
the first billion-dollar corporation in
history.

165
00:12:18,152 --> 00:12:21,585
It controlled about two thirds of the
steel produced in America.

166
00:12:22,384 --> 00:12:26,520
Now Morgan's empire stretched from
railroads to steel to electricity.

167
00:12:27,319 --> 00:12:33,695
The next step was a railroad trust called
Northern Securities, designed to end
competition across the northwest.

168
00:12:34,493 --> 00:12:36,937
But a new president had just taken office.

169
00:12:37,735 --> 00:12:43,760
Theodore Roosevelt looked at the biggest
bank in America and decided the government
had to be stronger than any banker.

170
00:12:44,558 --> 00:12:47,263
His administration sued to break up the
trust.

171
00:12:48,061 --> 00:12:52,406
Carnegie, as the story goes, later
regretted not asking for more.

172
00:12:53,204 --> 00:12:57,002
He told Morgan he should have demanded
another one hundred million dollars.

173
00:12:57,801 --> 00:13:00,700
Morgan answered that if he had, he would
have paid it.

174
00:13:01,498 --> 00:13:06,091
United States Steel was capitalized at
about one point four billion dollars.

175
00:13:06,889 --> 00:13:11,338
It was larger than the entire annual
budget of the American government at the
time.

176
00:13:12,136 --> 00:13:14,789
Morgan had built the largest company on
earth.

177
00:13:15,587 --> 00:13:20,453
He had done it by merging his rivals, and
then putting his own men at the head of
the board.

178
00:13:21,251 --> 00:13:24,203
This is why the banker was both admired
and feared.

179
00:13:25,001 --> 00:13:31,872
He had used his power to bring order, but
a lot of ordinary Americans could only see
a man who could decide the price of steel,

180
00:13:32,032 --> 00:13:34,684
the cost of freight, and the fate of the
economy.

181
00:13:35,483 --> 00:13:37,067
Morgan was furious.

182
00:13:37,866 --> 00:13:42,171
He traveled to the White House and told
Roosevelt: "If we have done something
wrong,

183
00:13:42,397 --> 00:13:44,958
send your man to my man and they can fix
it up."

184
00:13:45,756 --> 00:13:48,682
Roosevelt answered that the law did not
work like that.

185
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:51,716
The Supreme Court dissolved the trust.

186
00:13:52,514 --> 00:13:54,984
For the first time, Morgan had lost.

187
00:13:55,782 --> 00:13:57,992
And his greatest test was still ahead.

188
00:13:58,926 --> 00:14:05,563
In October of nineteen oh-seven, a failed
scheme to corner the copper market sent
shockwaves through New York's banks.

189
00:14:06,363 --> 00:14:09,198
One trust company after another faced
runs.

190
00:14:09,996 --> 00:14:15,174
Customers lined up around the block to
withdraw their money, and the whole system
began to buckle.

191
00:14:15,973 --> 00:14:17,596
There was no central bank.

192
00:14:18,394 --> 00:14:20,591
There was no federal fund to step in.

193
00:14:21,389 --> 00:14:25,916
So New York looked to the seventy-year-old
man in the library on Thirty-Sixth Street.

194
00:14:26,715 --> 00:14:28,416
Morgan worked day and night.

195
00:14:29,215 --> 00:14:35,395
He sent his partners to examine the books
of the failing banks, deciding which could
be saved and which had to fall.

196
00:14:36,194 --> 00:14:39,419
He organized the city's bankers into
rescue pools.

197
00:14:40,217 --> 00:14:43,195
He persuaded the Treasury to deposit
federal money.

198
00:14:43,993 --> 00:14:45,669
Then came the famous night.

199
00:14:46,467 --> 00:14:51,476
When the bankers could not agree on a
final plan, Morgan locked them inside his
library,

200
00:14:51,689 --> 00:14:54,836
pocketed the key, and refused to let
anyone leave.

201
00:14:55,634 --> 00:14:57,427
Near dawn, they signed.

202
00:14:58,225 --> 00:14:59,484
The panic stopped.

203
00:15:00,282 --> 00:15:02,231
The stock market stabilized.

204
00:15:03,030 --> 00:15:06,541
The economy was saved, again by a single
private man.

205
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:11,333
It began on October twenty-second, when
the Knickerbocker Trust failed.

206
00:15:12,131 --> 00:15:18,481
The panic spread so fast that by the end
of that week, some of the biggest banks in
New York had no cash left.

207
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:23,976
Morgan ordered his partners to inspect the
books of the failing Trust Company of
America.

208
00:15:24,775 --> 00:15:28,586
By midnight, he had learned it could be
saved, and that if it fell,

209
00:15:28,746 --> 00:15:30,278
the whole city would follow.

210
00:15:31,077 --> 00:15:37,283
Treasury Secretary George Cortelyou rushed
to New York and deposited millions of
federal dollars into the banks.

211
00:15:38,082 --> 00:15:38,820
John D.

212
00:15:38,915 --> 00:15:43,716
Rockefeller, the world's richest man,
added ten million dollars of his own.

213
00:15:44,514 --> 00:15:46,203
But the money was not enough.

214
00:15:47,001 --> 00:15:49,093
Morgan had to organize the rest.

215
00:15:49,892 --> 00:15:58,481
When the stock exchange itself was within
hours of closing, Morgan quietly arranged
for twenty-five million dollars in loans
so that trading could continue.

216
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:03,156
And when a brokerage firm was about to
collapse, taking the market with it,

217
00:16:03,394 --> 00:16:07,895
he struck a deal with Roosevelt to allow
United States Steel to absorb a rival,

218
00:16:08,004 --> 00:16:09,796
Tennessee Coal and Iron.

219
00:16:10,595 --> 00:16:19,211
But even as New York celebrated, a darker
conclusion spread across Washington: a
country that needed one banker to survive
was a country with a problem.

220
00:16:20,009 --> 00:16:21,515
The backlash was coming.

221
00:16:22,446 --> 00:16:24,994
The backlash began in nineteen twelve.

222
00:16:25,795 --> 00:16:31,220
A congressional committee, led by Arsène
Pujo, investigated what it called the
money trust:

223
00:16:31,394 --> 00:16:35,452
a tiny circle of bankers who controlled
the flow of credit across the country.

224
00:16:36,250 --> 00:16:41,142
Morgan, seventy-five years old and in
failing health, was called to testify.

225
00:16:41,940 --> 00:16:46,598
When the lawyer asked whether a man could
borrow without owning property, Morgan
answered,

226
00:16:46,745 --> 00:16:48,369
"The first thing is character.

227
00:16:49,167 --> 00:16:53,564
A man I do not trust could not get money
from me on all the bonds in Christendom."

228
00:16:54,362 --> 00:16:57,978
It was a memorable line, but the country
was no longer listening.

229
00:16:58,776 --> 00:17:01,650
It had seen how much power one man could
hold.

230
00:17:02,448 --> 00:17:04,892
That same year, another blow landed.

231
00:17:05,690 --> 00:17:10,322
The Titanic, the greatest ship of the age,
sank on its first voyage.

232
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:13,069
Morgan's shipping company owned it.

233
00:17:13,868 --> 00:17:17,887
He had reportedly planned to sail on it,
and cancelled at the last minute.

234
00:17:18,685 --> 00:17:22,132
The following spring, Morgan traveled to
Europe to rest.

235
00:17:22,930 --> 00:17:28,356
On March thirty-first, nineteen thirteen,
he died in Rome, at seventy-five.

236
00:17:29,154 --> 00:17:31,858
Even in that spotlight, he kept
collecting.

237
00:17:32,657 --> 00:17:35,777
Morgan was one of the greatest art
collectors in history.

238
00:17:36,576 --> 00:17:47,392
He bought Renaissance paintings, medieval
manuscripts, and a Gutenberg Bible, and he
filled a private library next to his house
with treasures that now belong to the
public as the Morgan Library.

239
00:17:48,190 --> 00:17:50,478
The reaction in Washington was swift.

240
00:17:51,276 --> 00:17:57,340
In December of nineteen thirteen, nine
months after he died, Congress passed the
Federal Reserve Act,

241
00:17:57,513 --> 00:18:02,158
creating a central bank so that the
country would never again need a single
banker to save it.

242
00:18:02,956 --> 00:18:04,384
His son, J.P.

243
00:18:04,297 --> 00:18:08,004
Morgan Junior, inherited the bank, and the
family fortune.

244
00:18:08,802 --> 00:18:11,754
But the era of the one-man central bank
was over.

245
00:18:12,552 --> 00:18:14,163
The twist was in his will.

246
00:18:14,961 --> 00:18:19,423
His estate was valued at under one hundred
million dollars, much of it in art.

247
00:18:20,222 --> 00:18:24,293
Carnegie is said to have remarked, "And to
think he wasn't even a rich man."

248
00:18:25,091 --> 00:18:30,009
The banker who seemed to own America, in
the end, had barely owned a fraction of
it.

249
00:18:30,942 --> 00:18:31,524
J.P.

250
00:18:31,437 --> 00:18:33,881
Morgan was not the richest man of his era.

251
00:18:34,681 --> 00:18:36,943
Rockefeller and Carnegie were richer.

252
00:18:37,741 --> 00:18:41,904
But he was the most powerful, because he
understood something that they did not:

253
00:18:42,103 --> 00:18:45,875
that in a modern economy, control does not
come from owning things.

254
00:18:46,673 --> 00:18:49,365
It comes from being trusted with other
people's money.

255
00:18:50,163 --> 00:18:52,503
His story holds a warning and a gift.

256
00:18:53,301 --> 00:18:59,117
The warning: a country that needs one
private man to save it is a country that
has given away its safety.

257
00:18:59,916 --> 00:19:07,060
After nineteen oh-seven, the United States
built the Federal Reserve, so that no
single banker would ever hold the keys
again.

258
00:19:07,858 --> 00:19:11,708
The gift: character, he said, is the real
collateral.

259
00:19:12,507 --> 00:19:18,036
The man who locked the door and saved a
nation also showed the nation why it
should never need him again.

260
00:19:18,835 --> 00:19:23,857
He lived in a time when the line between
public power and private power barely
existed.

261
00:19:24,655 --> 00:19:29,859
Morgan stood on that line, and for a few
decades, he decided where the country
would go.

262
00:19:30,658 --> 00:19:31,865
That is the lesson.

263
00:19:32,663 --> 00:19:39,039
Power built on trust can build a nation,
and it can also make that nation
uncomfortably dependent on a single
person.

264
00:19:39,837 --> 00:19:45,354
Subscribe, and join me next time for
another story of money, power and
consequence.

